  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote the tower-stacking game in `src/main.ts`. It typechecks, but I couldn't run it in a browser here, so the rendering and the camera follow are untested.\n\n- **Gameplay:**\n  - A slab slides along x above the tower. Space or a canvas click drops it.\n  - Only the overlap stays, and the cut-off piece spins and falls away.\n  - A drop within 0.25 units of centre keeps the full width and adds a bonus point.\n  - A drop with no overlap sets `lost`; reaching 25 stacked slabs sets `won`.\n  - Speed rises by 0.8 every 3 stacked slabs. Slab colour shifts a little each level.\n- **Restart:** R restarts at any time. A click on the canvas restarts once the game is over.\n- **Rendering:** Slabs and falling pieces are two instanced box nodes with per-instance colours. A perspective camera follows the tower top, moved each frame with `app.camera?.setPose`. If `app.camera` is undefined the camera simply won't move.\n- **HUD:** A DOM bar under the canvas shows score, best score and status. `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated on every change, and `window.__TASK_READY__` is set after the first frames are drawn.\n- **Height:** I defined `height` as the number of slabs stacked on top of the base slab. It starts at 0, matching the starting score of 0, and `won` triggers at `height` 25. The task didn't say whether the base counts, so say so if you want it counted.\n- **Score:** Score is stacked slabs plus 1 per perfect drop.\n\n",
